    "points": 6,
    "timeMinutes": null,
    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "In the diagram, $RSP$ is a straight line and $\\angle QSP = 80^\\circ$. The measure of $\\angle PQR$ is",
    "image": "qimages/cemc/gauss8-2008/q13.webp",
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "$120^\\circ$"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "$90^\\circ$"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "$80^\\circ$"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "$60^\\circ$"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "$75^\\circ$"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "B",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Two isosceles triangles",
        "body": "The tick marks show $RS = SQ = SP$. $\\angle RSQ = 180^\\circ - 80^\\circ = 100^\\circ$, so in isosceles $\\triangle RSQ$, $\\angle RQS = \\frac{180^\\circ - 100^\\circ}{2} = 40^\\circ$. In isosceles $\\triangle PSQ$, $\\angle PQS = \\frac{180^\\circ - 80^\\circ}{2} = 50^\\circ$. So $\\angle PQR = 40^\\circ + 50^\\circ = 90^\\circ$.\n\n**Answer: (B) $90^\\circ$**"
      },
      {
        "title": "Angle sum",
        "body": "Let $\\angle R = \\angle RQS = a$ and $\\angle P = \\angle PQS = b$. In $\\triangle PQR$: $a + b + (a + b) = 180^\\circ$, so $\\angle PQR = a + b = 90^\\circ$ (whatever the $80^\\circ$ is).\n\n**Answer: (B) $90^\\circ$**"
      }
    ],
    "topics": [
      "geometry"
    ],
    "difficulty": 2,
    "duplicateOf": null,
    "reviewNote": null
  },
