    "points": 8,
    "timeMinutes": null,
    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "A $3 \\times 3$ grid is filled with the digits 1, 2 and 3 so that each number appears once in each row and column. Two different examples are\n\n$$\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|}\\hline 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ \\hline 3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ \\hline 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ \\hline \\end{array} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\begin{array}{|c|c|c|}\\hline 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ \\hline 2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ \\hline 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ \\hline \\end{array}$$\n\nHow many different ways are there of filling the grid?",
    "image": null,
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "14"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "12"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "10"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "8"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "6"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "B",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "First row, then first column",
        "body": "There are $3! = 6$ choices for the first row. Once the first row is fixed (say 1, 2, 3), the first column starts with 1 and the rest of it is 2, 3 or 3, 2 — 2 choices. Each choice then forces the rest of the grid in exactly one way.\n\nTotal: $6 \\times 2 = 12$.\n\n**Answer: (B) 12**"
      },
      {
        "title": "Place the 1s",
        "body": "The three 1s must be in different rows and columns: $3! = 6$ placements. The remaining squares in each row then hold a 2 and a 3; choosing where the 2 goes in the first row forces everything else, giving 2 choices. $6 \\times 2 = 12$.\n\n**Answer: (B) 12**"
      }
    ],
