  {
    "id": "cemc-gauss8-2008-25",
    "bank": "cemc",
    "source": {
      "contest": "Gauss Contest (CEMC)",
      "paper": "Grade 8",
      "year": 2008,
      "number": 25,
      "url": "https://cemc.uwaterloo.ca/sites/default/files/documents/2008/2008Gauss8Contest.pdf"
    },
    "grade": "8",
    "points": 8,
    "timeMinutes": null,
    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "Chantelle had two candles, one of which was 32 cm longer than the other. She lit the longer one at 3 p.m. and lit the shorter one at 7 p.m. At 9 p.m., they were both the same length. The longer one was completely burned out at 10 p.m. and the shorter one was completely burned at midnight. The two candles burned at different, but constant, rates. What was the sum of the original lengths of the two candles?",
    "image": null,
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "42 cm"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "48 cm"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "60 cm"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "80 cm"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "52 cm"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "E",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Compare the rates",
        "body": "At 9 p.m. the candles are equal. The long one burns that length in 1 hour, the short one in 3 hours, so the long one burns 3 times as fast. Let the short one burn $x$ cm/h and the long one $3x$.\\n\\nBy 9 p.m. the long candle has burned $6 \\times 3x = 18x$ and the short one $2x$. The long one started 32 cm longer, so $18x - 2x = 32$, giving $x = 2$.\\n\\nShort candle: burned 5 hours at 2 cm/h, length 10 cm. Long candle: 7 hours at 6 cm/h, length 42 cm. Sum: 52 cm.\n\n**Answer: (E) 52 cm**"
      },
      {
        "title": "Use the 9 p.m. length",
        "body": "Let $L$ be the common length at 9 p.m. The long candle burns $L$ per hour (gone in 1 hour), so it started at $L + 6L = 7L$. The short one burns $\\frac{L}{3}$ per hour, so it started at $L + \\frac{2L}{3} = \\frac{5L}{3}$. Then $7L - \\frac{5L}{3} = \\frac{16L}{3} = 32$, so $L = 6$, and the lengths are 42 and 10: sum 52 cm.\n\n**Answer: (E) 52 cm**"
      }
    ],
    "topics": [
      "rates",
